    {
      "part": "Unit Conversion",
      "number": "2",
      "title": "Unit conversion table",
      "commentary": [],
      "tables": [
        {
          "head": [
            "Quantity",
            "Conversion"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Distance",
              "1 nautical mile (nm) = 1852 m = 1.852 km (international definition, fixed in 1929)"
            ],
            [
              "Speed",
              "1 knot (kn) = 1 nm/h = 1.852 km/h = 0.5144 m/s"
            ],
            [
              "Distance (short)",
              "1 cable = 0.1 nautical mile = 185.2 m"
            ],
            [
              "Length",
              "1 foot (ft) = 0.3048 m;  1 inch (in) = 2.54 cm;  1 yard (yd) = 0.9144 m"
            ],
            [
              "Depth (English charts)",
              "1 fathom (fm) = 6 feet = 1.8288 m"
            ],
            [
              "Temperature",
              "°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9;  °F = °C × 9/5 + 32"
            ],
            [
              "Pressure",
              "1 hPa = 1 mbar;  1 mmHg ≈ 1.333 hPa;  1 inHg ≈ 33.86 hPa"
            ],
            [
              "Mass (older ship documents)",
              "1 long ton (LT) = 1016 kg;  1 metric tonne = 1000 kg"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "part": "Visibility Range",
      "number": "3",
      "title": "Distance to the horizon by height of eye",
      "commentary": [
        "Simplified formula (including standard terrestrial refraction): d (nm) ≈ 2.08 × √h (m), where h is the observer's height of eye above sea level.",
        "For the range at which a light or object becomes visible (not just the horizon), the observer's and the object's ranges add together: d = d(h_observer) + d(h_object) — the \"geographical range\"; the actual detection range is also limited by the light's intensity and atmospheric clarity (luminous-range diagram/table)."
      ],
      "tables": [
        {
